---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import MetricCard from '../../components/MetricCard.astro';
import RollingMaeChart from '../../components/RollingMaeChart.astro';
import PredictionQualityChart from '../../components/PredictionQualityChart.astro';
import PermutationImportanceChart from '../../components/PermutationImportanceChart.astro';

const systemSteps = [
  'ESPN JSON endpoints',
  'Validation + SQLite',
  'Feature Engineering',
  'Model Training',
  'Evaluation + Reports',
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="NBA Player Stat Prediction — Liam Kinnally"
  description="Technical project page for Liam Kinnally's NBA player-stat prediction pipeline built with Python, scikit-learn, SQLAlchemy, pytest, and GitHub Actions."
>
  <article class="project-page container-shell pt-32 pb-24">
    <a href="/" class="text-sm font-semibold text-slate-300 transition hover:text-white">← Back home</a>

    <header class="mt-12 max-w-4xl">
      <div class="eyebrow">Machine learning · data engineering · testing</div>
      <h1
        class="scroll-heading-shine gradient-text mt-6 text-5xl font-[780] leading-[1.02] tracking-[-0.05em] sm:text-6xl"
        data-scroll-heading
        data-shine-text="NBA Player Stat Prediction"
      >NBA Player Stat Prediction</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-lg leading-8 text-slate-300 sm:text-xl">
        Predicting how many points, assists, and rebounds an NBA player will record in his next game — using only what was known before tipoff. An end-to-end Python pipeline covering data collection, feature engineering, model training, and evaluation against a simple baseline.
      </p>
      <div class="mobile-initial-highlight-group mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
        <span class="kbd-tag">Python</span><span class="kbd-tag">scikit-learn</span><span class="kbd-tag">SQLAlchemy</span><span class="kbd-tag">SQLite</span><span class="kbd-tag">Matplotlib</span><span class="kbd-tag">pytest</span><span class="kbd-tag">GitHub Actions</span>
      </div>
      <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <a href="https://github.com/liamkinnally/nba-scoring-model" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" class="button-primary">View source ↗</a>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="mt-16 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4">
      <MetricCard value="1,230" label="Games ingested" />
      <MetricCard value="26,547" label="Player-game rows" />
      <MetricCard value="4.85" label="Points MAE" detail="average miss per prediction" />
      <MetricCard value="23 / 28" label="7-day windows won" detail="points model vs. baseline" />
    </section>

    <section class="mt-24 grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[0.72fr_1.28fr]">
      <h2
        class="scroll-heading-shine text-2xl font-bold tracking-[-0.03em]"
        data-scroll-heading
        data-shine-text="The question"
      >The question</h2>
      <div class="space-y-5 text-lg leading-8 text-slate-300">
        <p>
          How well can you predict an NBA player's next box score — points, assists, rebounds — from only what was known before tipoff?
        </p>
        <p>
          Not perfectly, and the data shows why. Among games reserved for the model’s final evaluation, players who entered averaging 19–21 points over their previous ten games went on to score anywhere from 0 to 41, and only about half finished within five points of that average. Recent scoring, minutes, rest, and matchup history all carry useful signal, but none of them makes the next line certain.
        </p>
        <p>
          That natural variation makes a low average error hard to achieve. But a low error can also be easy to fake: a single feature that quietly peeks at the game being predicted will produce a number that looks excellent and means nothing. So the result only means something if it comes from an honest test — features that can only use information from the past, evaluation in the same order the games actually happened, and a simpler method held to the exact same conditions.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-24">
      <div class="mb-8">
        <div class="eyebrow">System design</div>
        <h2
          class="scroll-heading-shine mt-4 text-3xl font-bold tracking-[-0.035em]"
          data-scroll-heading
          data-shine-text="From source data to reproducible evaluation."
        >From source data to reproducible evaluation.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="scroll-frame-focus scroll-frame-quiet glass-card rounded-2xl p-6 sm:p-8">
        <div class="grid gap-3 md:grid-cols-5">
          {systemSteps.map((step, i) => (
            <div class="stationary-highlight scroll-frame-focus scroll-frame-quiet relative rounded-xl border border-white/[0.08] bg-white/[0.025] p-4">
              <div class="text-xs font-bold text-blue-200">0{i + 1}</div>
              <div class="mt-3 text-sm font-semibold text-slate-200">{step}</div>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-24 grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[0.72fr_1.28fr]">
      <h2
        class="scroll-heading-shine text-2xl font-bold tracking-[-0.03em]"
        data-scroll-heading
        data-shine-text="Keeping the test honest"
      >Keeping the test honest</h2>
      <div class="space-y-5 text-lg leading-8 text-slate-300">
        <p>
          A model that sees any part of the result it is supposed to predict will look accurate for the wrong reason. That failure is called data leakage, and preventing it shaped the whole pipeline.
        </p>
        <p>
          Every historical feature is built with a strict cutoff at the game being predicted. Jamal Murray’s 5- and 10-game scoring averages, his recent minutes, his rest, and how his team has been playing can only draw on games that finished earlier. Picture a spreadsheet with one row per appearance in date order: the row for Game 11 can use information from Games 1–10, but never anything from Game 11 itself. That cutoff is enforced by the date in the database query rather than by assuming the rows are already in the right order. Averages also include only games the player actually appeared in, so a night off is not treated as a zero.
        </p>
        <p>
          The same chronological rule applies to training. The most recent 20% of game dates are set aside completely for the final test. On the earlier data, hyperparameter tuning uses expanding-window cross-validation: the dates are divided into four consecutive blocks, producing three rounds where the model trains on everything earlier and validates on the next block. Each round gives the model more past data to learn from, but never lets a later game inform a prediction about an earlier one. That keeps the experiment close to the real task — using only what was known before tipoff to predict a player’s next line.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-24">
      <div class="mb-9 grid gap-8 lg:grid-cols-[0.72fr_1.28fr]">
        <h2
          class="scroll-heading-shine text-2xl font-bold tracking-[-0.03em]"
          data-scroll-heading
          data-shine-text="Evaluation"
        >Evaluation</h2>
        <div class="space-y-5 text-lg leading-8 text-slate-300">
          <p>
            A model can look accurate on its own terms and still add nothing if a much simpler rule does the same job. So the comparison is head-to-head: the tuned gradient boosting models against a 10-game rolling average, which predicts a player's next game from their recent form and nothing else.
          </p>
          <p>
            The final test uses that chronological holdout: 5,645 player-game rows from March 11, 2026 onward, none of them used in training. Across that full set, the points model’s mean absolute error is 4.85 — meaning its predictions miss by a little under five points on average. The head-to-head comparison uses the 5,612 rows where the baseline can also predict, since a player with no prior games has no recent form to average from. There, the model averaged 4.841 against 4.883 for the rolling average, and assists 1.407 against 1.417. Rebounds were effectively a tie.
          </p>
          <p>
            That margin is small, and it should be. A 10-game average is a strong baseline here because most players are reasonably consistent, so the honest claim isn't that the model is dramatically better — it's that it is reliably a little better. Across 28 trailing seven-day windows, it posted the lower error in 23 for points and 22 for assists. The edge also grew with scoring volume: 0.23% for players under 8 points per game, rising to 1.81% for players averaging 22 or more.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="mb-3 text-xs leading-5 text-slate-500">All visualizations on this page are rebuilt natively from the project’s generated reporting data.</p>
      <RollingMaeChart animateHeading interactivePill />
    </section>

    <section class="mt-28">
      <div class="mb-9 grid gap-8 lg:grid-cols-[0.72fr_1.28fr]">
        <div>
          <div class="eyebrow">Prediction quality</div>
          <h2
            class="scroll-heading-shine mt-4 text-2xl font-bold tracking-[-0.03em]"
            data-scroll-heading
            data-shine-text="Useful signal, with a visible ceiling."
          >Useful signal, with a visible ceiling.</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="space-y-5 text-lg leading-8 text-slate-300">
          <p>
            The chronological holdout contains 5,645 player-game observations. The model tracks typical scoring outcomes reasonably well, but predictions compress toward the middle instead of following extreme performances all the way up.
          </p>
          <p>
            That limitation is especially clear on ceiling games: every one of the 188 holdout performances of 30+ points was underpredicted, with an average underprediction of 13.9 points.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <PredictionQualityChart />
    </section>

    <section class="mt-28">
      <div class="mb-9 grid gap-8 lg:grid-cols-[0.72fr_1.28fr]">
        <div>
          <div class="eyebrow">Model interpretability</div>
          <h2
            class="scroll-heading-shine mt-4 text-2xl font-bold tracking-[-0.03em]"
            data-scroll-heading
            data-shine-text="Recent scoring drives the model."
          >Recent scoring drives the model.</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="space-y-5 text-lg leading-8 text-slate-300">
          <p>
            Holdout permutation importance measures how much prediction error increases when one feature is shuffled while the others stay intact. The player's 10-game scoring average dominates the points model, with recent minutes the strongest secondary signal.
          </p>
          <p>
            Because several rolling features are correlated, these values describe the contribution of each feature given the others rather than a complete measure of the underlying basketball concept's importance.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <PermutationImportanceChart />
    </section>

    <section class="mt-28 grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[0.72fr_1.28fr]">
      <h2
        class="scroll-heading-shine text-2xl font-bold tracking-[-0.03em]"
        data-scroll-heading
        data-shine-text="Engineering, not just modeling"
      >Engineering, not just modeling</h2>
      <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
        <div class="stationary-highlight scroll-frame-focus scroll-frame-quiet scroll-frame-leaf glass-card rounded-xl p-5"><h3 class="font-semibold">Reproducible artifacts</h3><p class="mt-2 leading-7 text-slate-300">Serialized model pipelines include evaluation-window metadata so reported metrics can be reproduced later.</p></div>
        <div class="stationary-highlight scroll-frame-focus scroll-frame-quiet scroll-frame-leaf glass-card rounded-xl p-5"><h3 class="font-semibold">Automated testing</h3><p class="mt-2 leading-7 text-slate-300">A pytest suite runs in GitHub Actions CI to catch regressions in the data and modeling pipeline.</p></div>
        <div class="stationary-highlight scroll-frame-focus scroll-frame-quiet scroll-frame-leaf glass-card rounded-xl p-5"><h3 class="font-semibold">CLI workflow</h3><p class="mt-2 leading-7 text-slate-300">Evaluation reports are regenerated from saved models in one command instead of relying on a notebook-only workflow.</p></div>
        <div class="stationary-highlight scroll-frame-focus scroll-frame-quiet scroll-frame-leaf glass-card rounded-xl p-5"><h3 class="font-semibold">Database-backed pipeline</h3><p class="mt-2 leading-7 text-slate-300">SQLAlchemy and SQLite provide a structured persistence layer for game and player-level records.</p></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="scroll-frame-focus scroll-frame-major mt-24 rounded-2xl border border-blue-200/10 bg-blue-200/[0.035] p-7 sm:p-9">
      <div class="eyebrow">Next</div>
      <h2
        class="scroll-heading-shine mt-4 text-2xl font-bold tracking-[-0.03em]"
        data-scroll-heading
        data-shine-text="See the implementation."
      >See the implementation.</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl leading-7 text-slate-300">The repository contains the data pipeline, feature engineering, model training, automated tests, evaluation CLI, and generated reporting workflow.</p>
      <a href="https://github.com/liamkinnally/nba-scoring-model" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" class="button-primary mt-6">Open GitHub repository ↗</a>
    </section>
  </article>

  <script>
    const headings = document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-scroll-heading]');
    const reducedMotion = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;

    if (reducedMotion || !('IntersectionObserver' in window)) {
      headings.forEach((heading) => heading.classList.add('is-visible'));
    } else {
      const observer = new IntersectionObserver(
        (entries) => {
          for (const entry of entries) {
            if (!entry.isIntersecting) continue;
            entry.target.classList.add('is-visible');
            observer.unobserve(entry.target);
          }
        },
        {
          threshold: 0.45,
          rootMargin: '0px 0px -8% 0px',
        },
      );

      headings.forEach((heading) => observer.observe(heading));
    }
  </script>
</BaseLayout>
